  "notes": "Bouyei (Haausqyaix), a Northern Tai language of Guizhou, in the official Latin orthography adopted in 1985 and based on the speech of Fuxing, Wangmo County. TONE LETTERS: as in the cognate Zhuang scheme, a syllable-final consonant letter spells the TONE, not a segment. The letters are l/q = 24, z = 11, c/j = 53, x/f = 31, s = 35, h/y = 33 and t = 35 (checked); the second letter of each pair spells the same tone in Chinese loanwords. A checked syllable with no tone letter is tone 8 (33), so the mapping adds that tone to a word-final stop coda. A tone letter is recognised word-finally and before a consonant letter, which is exactly where it can stand: a Bouyei syllable needs an onset, so a letter of the tone set that is not followed by a vowel is never an onset. This is why the map reads syllable-final l z c j x f s h y q t as tone and the same letters as l tɕʰ ts tsʰ tɕ ɕ f s x j tʰ in onset. INITIALS: b d g are the plain voiceless stops p t k; p t k are aspirated; mb nd are the implosives ɓ ɗ; by my gv ngv are the palatalised and labialised series; qy qv are preglottalised ʔj ʔv; r = z, h = x, hr = ɣ, sl = ɬ, x = ɕ, z = ts, c = tsʰ, j = tɕ, q = tɕʰ. A vowel-initial spelling carries a glottal onset. RIMES: the rime inventory is mapped whole rather than letter by letter, because vowel quality and length depend on the coda — a is a open but ɐ before a coda, aa is aː, o is o but ɔ before a coda, oo is oː, ee is e open and eː before a coda, e is ɯ, ea ei ie ue are ɯə ɯi iə uə, and ia ua become iə uə before a coda. Stop codas b d g are the unreleased p̚ t̚ k̚. Stress is not a contrastive category in Bouyei and is left unencoded (documented exemption). KNOWN LIMITS: ⟨qy⟩ is a genuine digraph, so a tone-letter ⟨q⟩ before an onset ⟨y⟩ is read as the preglottalised onset instead; and the apical vowel that Chinese loans have after s and z is not modelled, which the spelling gives no way to model. The 153-word WikiPron gold has one word in the environment, ⟨siyzij⟩ `s z̩ ˧ t͡s z̩ ˥˧`, returned here as si˧tsi˥˧. It also has three other ⟨si⟩ spellings — ⟨sis⟩, ⟨siqhuaq⟩, ⟨Siqcuany⟩ — whose nucleus is a plain /i/, so the sibilant does not condition the apical vowel; what conditions it is Chinese-loan status, which this orthography does not mark. The only string that would separate the four is the tone letter that follows the ⟨i⟩, and tone does not determine vowel quality, so a rule cut that way would be a lexical entry for one word wearing a rule's clothes. Left unmodelled deliberately. (The tie bar the gold writes on ⟨z⟩ is not a second error: the scorer folds tie bars, so ts and t͡s compare equal.) SOURCING: the official 1985 scheme (《布依文方案（修订案）》) could not be obtained. The letter inventory here follows the published Bouyei alphabet and rime charts compiled by Wolfram Siegel for Omniglot, and every value in it was checked against the 153-word WikiPron gold, where the letter-to-tone and letter-to-rime correspondences hold without exception. Length and the near-open quality of short a are written as the gold writes them, which is a measured notation rather than a sourced one. The tone CATEGORIES and their pitch values are corroborated independently by the SIL survey's Wangmo Fuxi data point, which gives 24 52 31 35 33 35 34 for tones 1 and 3 through 8 — agreeing with this map everywhere except tone 2, where that data point has 42 against the gold's 11.",
